A regional transit authority runs a rider app. A rider subscribes to any number of **route-directions** — there are about 1,400 of them — and the app's home screen shows every currently active disruption notice on the route-directions that rider subscribes to, newest first.

Dispatch staff publish notices from an operations console: delays, detours, cancelled trips, restored service. A weekday produces roughly 3,000 notices, but they do not arrive evenly. A wet Monday morning can put 40 notices out inside the same minute.

Subscriptions are far more lopsided than the notices. Each of the twenty busiest route-directions carries about 2 million subscribers. The median route-direction carries fewer than 400.

Reads dominate: the home screen is opened around 50 million times a day, with a sharp peak between 07:00 and 09:00 local time.

Design the notice delivery path and the home-screen read path. Cover at least:

- where the work of matching a notice to its subscribers is paid, and what one notice costs at each end of the subscriber distribution;
- the store the home screen reads from, the key it is partitioned on, and the latency target you are holding yourself to;
- what happens when a notice is edited or withdrawn twenty minutes after publication;
- behaviour during the forty-notices-in-a-minute peak: how late a notice is allowed to be, and how you would find out that it was late.

Take the rider-to-route subscription list and an authenticated app client as given. Device push transport is out of scope; the home screen is the target.

A marketplace pays its sellers out of a balance it keeps per seller. The card processor it uses reports every state change by calling an HTTP endpoint the marketplace owns:

- `charge.captured` — credit the seller's share to their balance
- `charge.refunded` — debit it back, possibly only in part
- `dispute.opened` — place a hold on the amount
- `dispute.closed` — release the hold or forfeit it

What the processor promises, and nothing beyond it:

- every event is delivered **at least once**; any call that does not return 2xx within five seconds is retried with backoff for 72 hours;
- each event carries a stable `event_id`, and the events about one charge carry a `seq` that increases by one per event on that charge;
- there is no ordering between different charges, and no bound on the gap between two deliveries of the same event — a duplicate landing three days later is ordinary;
- roughly 4,000 events per second on a normal evening, and about 25,000 per second in the first minutes of a sale.

Balances are read by sellers in their dashboard and by a nightly payout run. A balance that counts one capture twice is a cash loss the marketplace eats.

Design the receiving service. Cover at least:

- the endpoint contract: what must be true before it returns 2xx, and what it returns when it cannot handle the event right now;
- what makes an event a duplicate, where that knowledge is stored, how large that store grows, and when an entry may be dropped;
- the handling of a `charge.refunded` that arrives before the `charge.captured` it refunds;
- behaviour when the ledger database commits more slowly than events arrive;
- what happens when the ledger commit succeeds but the 2xx never reaches the processor.

Pickers in a warehouse carry handheld scanners. Each time an item is taken from a shelf the
scanner posts a `pick` event to a service that decrements that item's on-hand count.

What the scanners do, and nothing more:

- a scan is retried until the service answers 2xx — over patchy warehouse wifi that regularly
  means several deliveries of the same scan;
- each scan carries a `scan_id` generated on the device, stable across retries;
- a scanner that loses the network buffers locally and posts the backlog when it reconnects,
  which is routinely a few hundred scans at once and occasionally two hours' worth;
- about 90 scans a second across the site, several thousand in the minutes after a shift
  change.

The on-hand count feeds picking instructions and the replenishment trigger. A count that is
too low sends someone to restock a full shelf; a count that is too high sends a picker to an
empty one and stalls an order.

Design the receiving service. Cover at least:

- what must be true before the service answers 2xx, and what it answers when it cannot
  accept the scan right now;
- what makes a scan a duplicate, where that is stored, how big that store gets, and when an
  entry may be dropped;
- what happens to a two-hour backlog arriving at once from one scanner;
- how you would find out that the count has drifted from what is physically on the shelf.

You run a document rendering service. A customer posts a description and gets back a PDF. One entry point sits in front of 120 render workers.

What a render costs:

| statistic | render time |
|---|---|
| median | 45 ms |
| mean | 610 ms |
| 90th percentile | 900 ms |
| 99th percentile | 11 s |
| hard timeout | 90 s |

- peak is about 900 renders a second;
- a worker runs 8 renders at a time and queues anything beyond that;
- the pool is two hardware generations, and the older third is roughly 40% slower on the same document;
- every worker reads fonts and customer templates from one shared object store, and checks a licence against one internal service.

The pool does not hold still. A rolling deploy replaces all 120 workers over 20 minutes; autoscaling adds or drops around 15 workers on a five-minute cycle; and about once a week one worker ends up in a state where its renderer subprocess is dead, so it accepts the connection and returns a 500 in about 3 ms.

Design how work is distributed. Cover at least:

- the rule that picks a worker for an arriving render, and why it fits the cost distribution above rather than some other rule;
- what that rule does with the broken worker that answers in 3 ms;
- what the health probe tests, how often, and what the entry point does during the minute when the shared object store is degraded and every worker is failing its probe;
- what happens to renders already running on a worker that a deploy or a scale-in is about to take away;
- whether one customer's requests should keep landing on the same worker, and what would have to be true for that to be worth it.

A collaborative editor keeps each open document in memory on exactly one backend, so concurrent edits are merged without a round trip to storage. Editors arrive over WebSocket and stay for a work session: the median connection lasts 40 minutes, and the 95th percentile runs past six hours.

At the afternoon peak:

- 400 backends carrying about 1.6 million concurrent connections, so roughly 4,000 each;
- 700 thousand documents open at once, the median one with 2 people in it;
- a handful of published documents are opened read-only by 30 thousand people at a time;
- a backend holds about 1,700 documents and 6 GB of in-memory state;
- an edit must be applied and echoed to the other editors within 80 ms.

Operationally:

- the tier is deployed twice a week, rolling, one backend at a time;
- the cloud provider reclaims a backend without warning a few times a month;
- clients reconnect on their own as soon as a socket closes.

Design how a connection reaches a backend. Cover at least:

- how the entry point chooses a backend for a new connection, given that a document lives in exactly one place;
- what happens to that mapping when the pool goes from 400 backends to 401, and how many open documents move;
- how a document with 30 thousand read-only viewers is kept from sinking the single backend that owns it, without giving up single ownership of the edit path;
- what a failed health probe should mean for a backend holding 4,000 live connections;
- what becomes of those 4,000 connections during a rolling deploy, and what the tier looks like in the seconds after they all come back.
